docs {
  # PUT <uploadUrl> — the raw RTP upload (step 2 of 3, PAC-73)

  Not an API endpoint: this goes **straight to object storage** using the URL
  from `Presign Import`. Same three-step chain as
  `Deal Audits/Upload Audit Attachment`.

  - **Auth:** `none` — deliberately. The collection-level bearer would be sent
    on this request and **break the S3 signature**; a presigned URL carries its
    own credentials.

  ## Why this exists

  `Create Import` calls `HeadObject` on the declared key and 400s if nothing
  landed there, so the import cannot be exercised without a real upload. The
  declared `size` in the presign body is the client's claim; `HeadObject` is the
  only server-side evidence of what was actually stored.

  ## The body

  Two data rows, deliberately: one complete, and one with **no control number in
  either column** so the run reports a rejection with a reason rather than
  silently dropping it. The values are the real measured ones — all five money
  formats, `quotedate` `46216` (= 2026-07-13 under the 1899-12-30 Excel epoch),
  and `county` `017`, which must stay a zero-padded string.

  ## Headers
  `Content-Type` **must** match the signed value (`requiredHeaders` from the
  presign response) or storage rejects the PUT.

  ## Response
  - `200 OK` — object stored; the key can now be imported.
  - `403 Forbidden` — signature mismatch: usually an `Authorization` header
    being sent, a differing `Content-Type`, or an expired URL.
}
